  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nWe propose a hybrid optimization strategy combining Bayesian optimization with multi-resolution analysis to improve the current record for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The core idea is to:\n\n1. Start with the provided parent solution (n=480) and use Bayesian optimization with Gaussian Process surrogates for local refinement\n2. Use multi-start strategies to escape local optima by sampling different resolutions (n=360, 480, 720)\n3. Employ sophisticated probabilistic modeling of the objective function landscape\n4. Use dimensionality reduction to balance between solution quality and computational cost\n\nThe key bottleneck this approach addresses is the risk of local convergence in previous methods. By combining global exploration with local refinement and multi-resolution analysis, we should be able to find more significant improvements. However, the method is computationally intensive, requiring careful time allocation to avoid exceeding the 1300s budget.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nVariables:\n- Piecewise-constant function h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] represented by a vector of length n\n- n = number of samples (considered from {360, 480, 720})\n- sum(h) = n/2\n\nObjective function:\nC\u2085 = (1/dx) * max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nwhere dx = 2/n\n\nConstraints:\n1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n2. sum(h) = n/2\n3. The correlation must be evaluated over all valid lags ('full' mode)\n\nRepresentation:\n- h is represented as a NumPy array of length n\n- The spatial domain is [0,2] with points x_i = i * dx, dx = 2/n\n\nDerivations:\nWe need to compute the autocorrelation:\ncorr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\nThen C\u2085 = (dx * np.max(corr))\n\nWe use Bayesian optimization to minimize C\u2085 with the following search space:\n\n- Each dimension in [0,1] with a Gaussian Process kernel\n- Expected Improvement acquisition function\n- Box constraints preserved by projection\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - If initial_h_values is available, use it as a starting point\n   - Otherwise, initialize with normalized sine waves\n   - All initial candidates are projected to satisfy sum(h)=n/2\n\n2. Bayesian Optimization Loop:\n   for t in range(max_iterations):\n     1. Fit a Gaussian Process surrogate to the objective function\n     2. Compute expected improvement at all hyperparameter points\n     3. Select next point to evaluate using acquisition function\n     4. Evaluate candidate: compute C\u2085 and projection\n     5. Update acquisition function and surrogate\n     6. Limit function evaluations to scheduler budget\n\n3. Multi-Resolution Analysis:\n   Try different problem sizes (n=360,480,720):\n   - n=360: dx=2/360\u22480.0056, requires ~45% GA time\n   - n=480: dx=2/480\u22480.0042, requires parent solution\n   - n=720: dx=2/720\u22480.0028, requires strict time control\n\n4. Local Refinement:\n   After global search, perform up to global_min_iterations of projected SLSQP using the smooth_max_and_grad function with adaptive alpha\n\n5. Adaptation Control:\n   - Use a priority queue for different resolutions\n   - Start with n=480 (the parent)\n   - Move to higher resolutions if improvement is likely\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations:\n```python\nsearch_config = {\n    'n_values': [480] if 'initial_h_values' in globals() else [360, 480, 720],\n    'gp_kernel': 'Matern',\n    'max_global_evals': 100,  # Total global function evaluations\n    'time_limits': [900, 300, 0],  # Seconds per resolution\n    'alpha_schedule': [100, 500, 1000],\n    'local_iterations': [30, 60, 90],\n    'gp_bounds': {'nugget': [1e-6, 1e-4]},\n    'mcmc_mixture': True  # Important variation\n}\n```\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n- n=480 optimization: ~60% of budget (480s)\n- n=360 evaluation: ~20% (240s)\n- n=720 evaluation: ~15% (192s)\n- Local optimization: ~5% (65s)\n\nPriority order:\n1. n=480 (start with parent solution)\n2. n=360 if time remains (uses interpolation from parent)\n3. n=720 (theoretically higher resolution)\n\nFailure handling:\n- Use fallback local optimization even for infeasible solutions\n- Set minimal time_remaining check before expensive operations\n- If time < 10s, return best candidate with all constraints\n- Use exponential backoff for surrogate evaluation failures\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n- Candidates must satisfy:\n  np.alltrue(0 <= h[i] <= 1)\n  np.isclose(np.sum(h), n/2, atol=1e-5)\n- Validation checks:\n  check_overlap = compute_c5(best_h) < target_C5 + 1e-4\n  check_bounds = np.alltrue((0 <= best_h <= 1).astype(int))\n  check_sum = abs(np.sum(best_h) - n/2) < 1e-3 * n/2\n- Failure handling returns parent with interpolation\n\nThe algorithm uses a sophisticated Bayesian optimization framework with carefully tuned acquisition functions and Gaussian Process surrogates. Each candidate solution undergoes thorough projection and validation before acceptance. The multi-resolution prioritization ensures that we focus on the most promising candidates first, maximizing the chance of a record-breaking solution within the time budget.",
